KZ2 6083: the bounded NV406E ACQUIRE wait (GCM_SEMA_ACQUIRE=1 GCM_SEMA_ACQUIRE_MS=<ms>) is timed per
acquire and cleared when an acquire is met. The wait's start time was a static inside the wait, reset only when a wait
gave up: a met acquire left it standing, so the next unmet acquire with the same label and value inherited the start of
an earlier, already met wait and, once <ms> had passed since then, gave up at once without waiting. Killzone 2's frame
gate (vblank handler 1.29 0x48F40 writes label 0x49 = 1 every second call; the frame end 0x48580 acquires it at
0x486A8..0x486B8 and releases 0 at 0x486C4..0x486CC, before its flip at 0x487B4) has every acquire at label 0x49 == 1,
so about one frame in eight went through the gate unmet: an earlier change's gate run presented 34.0 frames a second against
~31.3 openings, and no frame took >= 100 ms (the give-up line is capped at 12, all at boot). Now the timer is file
scope, keyed on the acquire's FIFO offset, label and value, and cleared at a met acquire; every give-up is counted and
6081's [acq] line prints "gave up=N" per window. Only with GCM_SEMA_ACQUIRE=1 and GCM_SEMA_ACQUIRE_MS set (both off by
default); no default behaviour change. Upstream candidate (ps3recomp libs/video/cellGcmSys.c, same code).
Host test: tools/runtime/test_acq_bounded_wait.sh (the real code in a gate simulation, with a negative control).
Review fix: the label is read once per acquire (acq_have) and that one value decides both the met reset
and the wait, so a vblank write landing between two reads can no longer consume an acquire with its timer standing
(test_acq_bounded_wait check 4, with a control that re-reads).

